  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1259 PM CST Tue Nov 10 2026\n\nA couple shortwaves aloft combine into a longwave trof tomorrow and \nThursday. A sfc low and strong cold front swing through the region \nfrom the northwest tomorrow. Models continue to depict moisture \nadvecting in from central TX, keeping our dewpoints right around 57-\n58 degrees, which continues to be consistent with previous \nforecasts. Isolated to scattered pre-frontal showers/storms are \nstill possible mainly over southwest IN, and the KY Pennyrile \nWednesday afternoon. In an environment with about 400-600 J/kg of \nMLCAPE and about 40 kts of bulk shear, the SPC Day 2 Marginal Risk \n(level 1 of 5) makes sense, mainly for a damaging wind threat but \nalso a VERY conditional low-end tornado threat (will heavily depend \non over achieving low level moisture and instability). Given the \nrecent model consistency, would say that there is less than a 10% \nchance of moisture over performing. \n\nMuch colder air moves in behind the front to finish out the week. \nThis brings back chilly fall temperatures with highs struggling to \nreach 50 degrees on Friday. Breezy conditions are expected behind \nthe front on Thursday which will make the highs in the 50s feel \nchillier. Our first hard freeze (temperatures 28 degrees or less) \ncould happen Friday night and Saturday night. Temperatures then \nbegin to moderate slightly on Sunday with sfc ridging stemming from \nsfc high pressure over the western Great Lakes. Another shortwave \naloft approaches and passes through on Monday, though any convection \nlooks to remain south of us.",
